KNOWNBUG libstdcxx
main.cpp
--cpp17 --object-bits 12 --unwind 8 --no-unwinding-assertions
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ vector<int> deduced from a braced list: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
g++ and clang++ (GCC 16 for C++23 and C++26) accept this program, and it fails
only its WRONG assertion.  `std::vector v{1, 2, 3}' deduces std::vector<int,
int> and main fails to type-check (CONVERSION ERROR): the guide from the
initializer_list constructor, vector(initializer_list<T>, const Allocator & =
Allocator()), should be chosen ([over.match.list]/1 considers initializer-list
constructors first), deducing T = int and the default Allocator.  The class
template argument deduction of user-defined classes, std::pair and an
initializer_list constructor of a user class works.
